What this account may do
29-sd-tcl-the-terminal-and-the-session
Each row is a gate: something the administrator switches for an account. ON means this session holds it now; off means it does not. The row under the cursor is explained in plain words: what ON lets you do, what you can still do with it off, and the modify.account word the administrator would use. Nothing on this page can change a gate, and nothing is tried to find out: the page asks the system what the session may do.
Some gates are about the account (sh, os, sd, net and the shared account, loaded when you entered it) and some about you as a person (ssh, api, files, ai: membership of a Linux group by your login name). The edit gates (voc, bp, dict) are read once when you sign on; after the administrator changes one, sign off and on again to see it here. Every account starts with every gate off except the right to read the shared account.
